-- Passage rg8-literary-structure-pov-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('055b2607-5671-53ce-88c8-4e18850fa7f7', 'rg8-literary-structure-pov-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Banner in the Equipment Room$q$, $q$Every member of the Ridgeview cross-country team knew about the banner except the one person it was for. Coach Delgado was retiring after twenty-two seasons, and the runners had spent three weeks painting a huge sheet that read THANK YOU, COACH D. They hid it in the equipment room, rolled up behind the hurdles, because Coach never went in there. She always sent someone else.

On Friday afternoon, Wren was the lookout. Her job was simple: keep Coach on the track until four o'clock, when the families would arrive.

"Coach," Wren said, "could you check my stride? I think my left foot lands funny."

Coach Delgado watched her run one lap. "Your stride is fine," she said. "Actually, I need the stopwatch bag from the equipment room. I'll just grab it myself."

Wren's stomach dropped. Across the field, Tomasz froze with a roll of tape in his hand.

"No!" Wren said, louder than she meant to. "I mean, I'll get it. You should rest. You've been standing all day."

Coach raised an eyebrow. "I've been standing for twenty-two years, Wren. I think I can manage forty more steps."

"Then check Keiko's stride," Wren said quickly. "Her feet are way funnier than mine."

Keiko, who had been stretching nearby, looked up in confusion. Then she saw Wren's face and began to jog in a strange, wobbly way, swinging her arms like a windmill.

Coach Delgado stared at her for a long moment. "Keiko, what on earth are you doing?"

"Running," Keiko said. "Badly. On purpose. I mean, not on purpose."

Behind them, Tomasz slipped out of the equipment room with the stopwatch bag and tossed it toward Wren. Coach caught the bag first, turned it over in her hands, and smiled a small smile that none of the runners noticed.$q$, 'draft')
