   $q$Why does the poet include the lines "She was seventy-one years old" and "She dug one square, then stopped to rest" early in the poem?$q$, $q$[{"text":"They suggest the poem will be mainly about aging rather than about the lot.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-theme"},{"text":"They explain why the neighbors refused to help with the garden at any point.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"They show how small and slow the start was, which makes the later growth more striking.","is_correct":true},{"text":"They show that Mrs. Ito was too tired to help after the first day of digging.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$They show how small and slow the start was, which makes the later growth more striking.$q$, $q$One older woman digging one square is a tiny beginning, so the bees, beans and crowd in later stanzas feel bigger by contrast. Neighbors do join in the next stanza, so they did not refuse to help.$q$, 'draft', '8f1dbff1-9f04-534f-a72c-c1ab1455c97d')
